  "worked_solution": "Given a frequency table containing continuous data split into $K$ classes, the mean can be estimated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}≈\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{K}{f}_{i}\\cdot {m}_{i}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${f}_{i}$ is the frequency of the $i$th class,\n\n- ${m}_{i}$ is the midpoint of the $i$th class, and\n\n- $n$ is the total number of observations.\n\nTo estimate the mean, we represent the given data as a table and add columns for the midpoints of each class and the products of the midpoints and frequencies.\n\n| Rainfall ($\\text{mm}$) | ${m}_{i}$ | ${f}_{i}$ | ${m}_{i}\\cdot {f}_{i}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $200\\le r<250$ | $225$ | $3$ | $675$ |\n| $250\\le r<300$ | $275$ | $5$ | $1375$ |\n| $300\\le r<350$ | $325$ | $4$ | $1300$ |\n| $350\\le r<400$ | $375$ | $2$ | $750$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFinally, we sum the values in the last column and divide by $14$ (the total number of observations).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{r} & ≈\\frac{675+1375+1300+750}{14} \\\\  & =\\frac{4100}{14} \\\\  & ≈292.86\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
